{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 25, :comments ["<b>And behold, a caravan of Ishmaelites coming from Gilead</b> — when they lifted their eyes and saw from afar men coming by way of Gilead, they recognized by the camels that it was a caravan of Ishmaelites, and they knew that they were headed to Egypt, for from Gilead the gum (צרי) and the balm (נכאת) are brought, and to Egypt was their route to transport it. Therefore Judah said to them: behold, these men are from a distant land and are going to a far-off land — let us sell him to them, for the matter will not be known. And when they drew near to them, they found that they were merchants who had the balm and the gum — Midianite traders who had hired the camels from the Ishmaelites. And they sold Joseph to the Midianites who bought him to trade in him as merchandise, for a caravan of Ishmaelites who are hired camel-drivers would not purchase merchandise for themselves. And Scripture says (v. 28): \"And they sold Joseph to the Ishmaelites,\" because it was to them that they handed him over, since they are the ones who transport the merchandise to Egypt. And this is the meaning of \"from the hand of the Ishmaelites who had brought him down there\" (Genesis 39:1), for he was in their hand, but the Midianites were his owners and they traded in him — which is what it says: \"And the Midianites sold him into Egypt.\"\n\nAnd every act in Scripture is sometimes attributed to the one who commands it and sometimes to the agent who carries it out by his hand, as in the matter stated: \"all the great work of YHWH that He did\" (Deuteronomy 11:7), and elsewhere it is written: \"which Moses did before all Israel\" (Deuteronomy 34:12). So too it says: \"And all the work that King Solomon made was completed\" (I Kings 7:51), yet Hiram made it, as it is written: \"And he came to King Solomon and made all his work\" (I Kings 7:14). And concerning Joseph himself it says: \"all that they did there, he was the one doing it\" (Genesis 39:22) — Scripture attributes the act both to the one commanding it and to the one performing it.\n\nAnd Rabbi Abraham [Ibn Ezra] said (on v. 28) that the Midianites are called Ishmaelites, as Scripture says concerning the kings of Midian: \"for Ishmaelites they are\" (Judges 8:24). But this is not as he thought, for the verse that says (there): \"for golden earrings are theirs, for Ishmaelites they are\" alludes to the children of the East, for that war was theirs, as it is written: \"and all Midian and Amalek and the children of the East gathered together\" (Judges 6:33), and the children of the East are the Ishmaelites, for regarding all the sons of Abraham's concubines it says: \"and he sent them away from Isaac his son while he yet lived, eastward, to the land of the East\" (Genesis 25:6). It is also possible that the kings were Ishmaelites and they ruled over Midian — for why would Midian be called by the name of Ishmael their brother?\n\nThe correct reading in the plain sense of Scripture regarding Joseph is what we have said. But our Rabbis said (Genesis Rabbah 84:20) that he was sold many times over:"], :book :genesis, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 37, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T04:22:10.224934740Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}